  <monitor x="455" y="65" width="130" height="60" display="Population" precision="0" fontSize="12">count turtles</monitor><plot x="455" y="140" width="510" height="350" display="Population by strategy" xAxis="Ticks" yAxis="Agents" xMin="0.0" xMax="100.0" yMin="0.0" yMax="100.0" autoPlotX="true" autoPlotY="true" legend="true"><setup/><update/><pen display="Humanitarian" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-16777216" legend="true"><setup/><update>plot count turtles with [cooperate-with-same? and cooperate-with-different?]</update></pen><pen display="Ethnocentric" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-2674135" legend="true"><setup/><update>plot count turtles with [cooperate-with-same? and not cooperate-with-different?]</update></pen><pen display="Egoist" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-13345367" legend="true"><setup/><update>plot count turtles with [not cooperate-with-same? and not cooperate-with-different?]</update></pen><pen display="Traitorous" interval="1.0" mode="0" color="-10899396" legend="true"><setup/><update>plot count turtles with [not cooperate-with-same? and cooperate-with-different?]</update></pen></plot></widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[## O_4: Group-conditional PD with per-tick score reset
The only behavioral change from O_3 is resetting every agent's score to zero at the start of each tick. Reproduction therefore depends on this tick's payoffs, not accumulated lifetime payoffs. Group-conditional behavior is retained. Each agent has a group-tag (0 to 3) and two independent Boolean rules: cooperate-with-same? and cooperate-with-different?. Tags and rules are assigned randomly at setup. The payoff and demographic procedures stay the same.

| Strategy | Same group | Different group | Shape |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Humanitarian | Cooperate | Cooperate | Circle |
| Ethnocentric | Cooperate | Defect | Square |
| Egoist | Defect | Defect | Triangle |
| Traitorous | Defect | Cooperate | X |

Each player applies its own rule to the opponent's tag. Neither player knows the opponent's rule or learns from past payoffs. Offspring inherit the tag, both rules, original color, and strategy shape unchanged; scores reset to zero. No mutation occurs.

Group colors: tag 0 blue, 1 green, 2 orange, 3 violet. Agents always display their group color. Decisions compare group-tag, independently of display color. Shapes distinguish strategies. This combines the tag and its two conditional rules into the single conditional-behavior step; no new demographic mechanism is added.

## SPACE AND SCHEDULE
100 agents start at score zero on randomly selected distinct patches in a 33 by 33 wrapping world. Agents stay in place. Each neighbors4 pair plays once per tick; who ordering prevents double-counting. No same-patch or diagonal interaction occurs.

After games, every current agent gets one reproduction attempt in random order. Birth probability (%) = max(0, min(100, 12 + this tick's score)). A successful attempt produces one offspring on a randomly selected empty neighboring patch if one exists. Scores are not spent. Offspring start at score zero, do not play or reproduce until next tick, and are included in the subsequent independent 10% death chance. Extinction stops the run. Birth placement, birth baseline, and random death remain as in O_3; score persistence alone changes.

## PAYOFFS
Cooperation costs 1 and gives the opponent 3.
| Action | Cooperate | Defect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cooperate | 2, 2 | -1, 3 |
| Defect | 3, -1 | 0, 0 |

These are Hammond-Axelrod's cost/benefit increments scaled by 100. Resetting score to zero and adding a baseline of 12 at reproduction is equivalent to resetting reproductive potential to 12 before applying this tick's payoffs. This aligns payoff persistence with Hammond-Axelrod; the initial population and absence of immigration and mutation remain differences. No immigration or mutation is included here.

## DISPLAY
Agents retain their group colors throughout interactions. Patches remain white; game locations are not highlighted. The line plot tracks counts of the four strategies over ticks; line colors identify strategies, while agent colors identify groups. The population monitor shows total current count. Scores remain internal to the birth calculation and are not displayed. A persistent isolated agent has no games and stays at score zero. Strategy counts reflect births and deaths; their sum equals the total population.
]]></info>
